# zenresize with_mask cost across a tiny→large size ladder — the i16 mask path of #3 cannot pay on aarch64

date:    2026-08-29
commit:  da03f80 + the extended benches/mask_e2e.rs ladder (this commit)
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc:   rustc 1.98.0 (88d9e12ae 2026-08-18)
build:   bench profile (release), NO -C target-cpu=native, runtime dispatch (incant!)
threads: single-threaded resize; `-j 4` is the cargo build job cap, not a runtime knob
simd:    bench-simd-competitors OFF (no competitor crates in this bench)
bench:   nice -n 19 cargo bench -j 4 --bench mask_e2e   (two runs, 110.8 s and 180.4 s)
logs:    mask_e2e_ladder_aarch64_2026-08-29.run1.log / .run2.log
issue:   https://github.com/imazen/zenresize/issues/3

## Question

Issue #3 proposes applying masks on the i16 paths so `with_mask()` stops forcing
the f32 path ("avoids f32 conversion overhead for the common case of rounded
corners + solid background -> JPEG"). The 2026-08-27 run
(`mask_e2e_aarch64_2026-08-27.meta`) sampled only 800x600 and up and found the
f32 fallback was already faster at >= 1080p, with 800x600 the single point that
favoured i16 (+5.7% .. +10.2%). That left the crossover unlocated, and per the
size-sweep discipline a 3-point grid starting at 0.5 MP cannot separate per-call
fixed overhead from the per-pixel slope. This run adds the tiny/small end.

Both arms use the same RGBA8_SRGB `.srgb()` config (opaque gradient, Lanczos).
`no_mask` takes I16Srgb and `rounded_mask` takes F32 in every one of the nine
scenarios — printed by the bench and verified in both logs.

## Result (in-run paired ratio, rounded_mask/F32 vs no_mask/I16Srgb)

A POSITIVE percentage means the forced f32 fallback is slower, i.e. an i16 mask
path could win that much. A NEGATIVE percentage means f32 is already faster and
an i16 mask path would be a regression.

| scenario           | no_mask (I16Srgb) | rounded_mask (F32) | 95% CI run 1     | 95% CI run 2     |
|--------------------|-------------------|--------------------|------------------|------------------|
| 64x64 -> 32x32     | 48.9 µs / 97.5 µs | 50.6 µs / 105.1 µs | +2.9% .. +4.5%   | +3.0% .. +4.3%   |
| 128x128 -> 64x64   | 168.7 / 273.9 µs  | 170.9 / 283.2 µs   | +1.1% .. +2.3%   | +1.4% .. +5.3%   |
| 256x256 -> 128x128 | 634.0 / 962.9 µs  | 626.9 / 975.4 µs   | -2.1% .. -0.1%   | -0.7% .. +3.3%   |
| 512x512 -> 256x256 | 2.4 / 3.4 ms      | 2.3 / 3.4 ms       | -0.7% .. -0.0%   | -0.8% .. +2.8%   |
| 800x600 -> 400x300 | 6.6 / 7.7 ms      | 6.5 / 7.8 ms       | -2.2% .. +1.3%   | -0.6% .. +3.4%   |
| 1024x1024 -> 512²  | 9.5 / 10.3 ms     | 9.6 / 10.3 ms      | +1.0% .. +1.5%   | -1.4% .. +1.8%   |
| 1440x1080 -> 720x540 | 14.1 / 14.2 ms  | 14.1 / 14.1 ms     | -1.3% .. +1.8%   | -2.0% .. +1.3%   |
| 4K -> 1080p        | 81.9 / 81.8 ms    | 69.0 / 69.1 ms     | -16.7% .. -14.8% | -16.3% .. -14.7% |
| 1080p -> 4K up     | 80.3 / 80.4 ms    | 56.8 / 56.7 ms     | -30.2% .. -28.4% | -30.5% .. -28.5% |

(Two absolute figures per cell: run 1 / run 2. Run 1 shared the box with other
build agents; run 2 had it to itself. Only the in-run paired ratio is
comparable across runs — the absolutes drift, as the 64x64 cell shows.)

## Conclusion: do not build the i16 mask path on aarch64

Three regimes, both runs agreeing on all three:

1. **<= 128x128 input: i16 wins, by at most ~4%.** That is the ENTIRE cost of
   the f32 fallback (linearize + premultiply + f32 kernels + unpremultiply), so
   it is an upper bound on what an i16 mask path could recover: 1.7 µs at 64x64,
   2.2 µs at 128x128 in run 1.
2. **256x256 .. 1440x1080: a wash.** Every CI in run 2 straddles zero.
3. **>= 4K: f32 is 15-16% faster downscaling and 28-30% faster upscaling.**
   Moving masked resizes onto I16Srgb would cost +12.7 ms on a masked
   4K -> 1080p and +23.7 ms on a masked 1080p -> 4K.

So the change proposed by #3 would trade a <= 4% (<= 2.2 µs) win on thumbnails
for a 15-30% regression on masked 4K work, and it would also be less precise
(u8-premultiplied double rounding vs the f32 path's single rounding). It is not
justified by measurement on this architecture at any size.

## Correction to the 2026-08-27 record

The 800x600 -> 400x300 point does NOT reproduce. That run reported
+5.7% .. +10.2% (favouring i16) from a single sample; here it lands at
-2.2% .. +1.3% (run 1, CV 25-26%) and -0.6% .. +3.4% (run 2) — a wash both
times. The 08-27 meta's framing of 800x600 as the one i16-favouring size was an
artefact of that point's variance. The genuine i16-favouring band is
<= 128x128, and it is far too small to matter.

The two large anchors reproduce well across the two runs here and across the
08-27 run (4K -> 1080p: -11.4% .. -9.6% on 08-27 vs -16.3% .. -14.7% today;
1080p -> 4K: -29.1% .. -26.5% vs -30.5% .. -28.5%) — same sign and same order of
magnitude on a different day and a differently loaded box.

## Still not measured: x86-64

CLAUDE.md records I16Srgb as "~2x faster" than f32, an x86 (AVX2/AVX-512)
number. If that ratio holds on the 7950X, the f32 fallback there really would
cost ~2x on masked resizes and the i16 mask path could pay — the opposite
conclusion to this one. Run this bench on the 7950X before deciding the x86
half. It cannot be answered from this machine: Rosetta exposes SSE4.2 but no
AVX2/FMA, so `--target x86_64-apple-darwin` compiles the V3 kernels without
executing them (see CLAUDE.md), and Rosetta timings would be meaningless anyway.
